
-------------------------------------Part 1-------------------------------------

Q3:

1. Output: 6 because 2 * 3 = 6.

2. Output: 24 because 2 * 3 * 4 = 24.

3. Output: 6 because Shape.Area() runs using the reference type. This is early binding because it is decided at compile time.


Q4:

Shape.ToString() runs because Cube inherits from Shape.

ToString() uses dynamic binding because it is virtual in Object, but Area() uses static binding because it is not virtual.


Part 2: Dynamic Binding

Q7:

Doctor Output:

I am a Person.
ID: 1
Name: Ahmed
Age: 35
Specialty: Cardiology

Engineer Output:

I am a Person.
ID: 2
Name: Omar
Age: 28
Field: Software Engineering
Years Of Experience: 5

Greet() runs from Person because it is not virtual.

Display() runs from the derived class because it is virtual and overridden.


Q8:

The compiler gives an error because Display() is not virtual in Person.

We must use virtual in the base class before using override.


Part 3: Interfaces

Q9:

Not all vehicles can fly. For example, cars and ships cannot move up and down.

Interfaces allow each class to implement only the methods it needs.


Q12:

No, because IMoveable does not have MoveUp().

We need an IFlyable reference to call MoveUp().


Q13:

IVehicle inherits from both interfaces, so the class implementing it must implement all four methods.

This makes the code more organized.


Q14:

Yes, a class can implement an interface explicitly.

The method can only be called using an interface reference, not directly through the object.

Calling it directly gives a compile-time error.


Part 4: Comprehension / Theory

Q15:

Feature: Static Binding | Dynamic Binding

Keyword in base: None | virtual

Keyword in derived: new | override

Resolved at: Compile time | Runtime

Behavior via base reference: Base method runs | Derived method runs


Q16:

virtual allows overriding and runtime polymorphism.

new hides the base method and works without virtual because it is decided at compile time.